                <div><img src="~/assets/img/healthcare/healthcare-crm-4.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 mt-3 me-3 p-3" /></div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-8 Imgborder" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Healthcare CRM Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>
                <p>
                    Healthcare organizations may struggle with fragmented engagement data, manual follow-ups, disconnected systems, and inconsistent communication. A structured CRM strategy can address these challenges.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Scattered Engagement Data: Centralize relevant interaction and relationship information so authorized teams have better visibility.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Manual Follow-Ups: Automate tasks, reminders, and communication workflows to improve consistency.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Disconnected Systems: Integrate CRM with relevant applications to reduce data duplication and improve information flow.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Inconsistent Communication: Use standardized communication workflows to support timely and consistent engagement.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Limited Reporting: Create dashboards and reports that provide useful insights into engagement and operational performance.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />
                <h3><b>The Importance of Customization in Healthcare CRM</b></h3>
                <p>Healthcare organizations have different patient engagement models, referral processes, communication requirements, and service workflows. Customization helps the CRM support these unique processes.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Customize Engagement Processes: Align CRM workflows with the organization's patient and customer engagement journey.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Create Role-Based Workspaces: Give each team access to the information and actions relevant to their responsibilities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Automate Follow-Ups: Configure automated tasks, alerts, reminders, and communication workflows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Build Custom Dashboards: Provide teams with role-specific insights into engagement, referrals, service activities, and performance.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Gather User Feedback: Continuously refine CRM processes based on user feedback and operational results.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
